Connect restored configuration to a defensible service claim (@engineering-service-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-operating-histories-repair-resources-and-service-evidence.lingua#r_00D184950B7394E9C0B38294BB") { r_00ACDDD0A8E0E505FCE7A8CADC
**Read:** [Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ), selected section 5.5 transition passages on product identity, supporting products, documentation and readiness. They connect a verified product with the people and setting that must use it. The NASA context is guidance for comparison, not a universal certification rule. Reuse [Condition observations, diagnosis and prediction](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Condition-observations-diagnosis-and-prediction.lingua#r_4PJF2YX9VDRZMDF31M6C290BEP), [Failure investigation preserves chronology and alternatives](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Failure-investigation-preserves-chronology-and-alternatives.lingua#r_2YM12BJW2NZH0A7FAC0K9DZC68) and [Repair, restoration and return to service](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Repair-restoration-and-return-to-service.lingua#r_1RF5PD4EHJKFZ57X6RSFKXJK1X) for distinguishing observations, mechanisms and restored function.

The finite study observes every component state correctly and declares a successful repair immediately usable at the next slot. A real service claim needs evidence for both assumptions. Separate the latent physical state, the instrument or operator observation, the diagnosis, the intervention and the resulting functional measurement. If two units produce no output, shared power loss, shared configuration error and simultaneous independent failures can give the same coarse observation. Replacing one unit without inspecting the shared dependency does not distinguish those explanations.

For the document-service example, define acceptable output correctness and rate under stated input varieties, the exact hardware/software/configuration combination, and the operating conditions. A replaced unit may pass an isolated throughput check yet disagree with its partner about an input format. A restored support service can alter ordering, timing or reference data. Recheck the affected interface and the intended combined function. Identify untested conditions and preserve them in the claim instead of converting a few successful trials into a universal service-life statement.

Restoration also consumes knowledge and resources. A nominal spare is useful only if its identity, interface and condition are established. A diagnostic method is useful only if someone can perform and interpret it. Access, tools, current instructions and the return check may dominate the time to usable service. Put those delays into the observation history; distinguish absent evidence from evidence of absence. Keep the as-found state and sequence of changes so that future failures can be compared on a consistent basis.

**Work:** write two rival accounts of a loss of output, one involving a shared dependency and one a unit. Propose a benign observation that discriminates between them and state what would remain ambiguous. Then describe two restoration choices, the changed configuration, their resource needs and evidence for return. Compare those real information needs with the model's perfect-state-observation assumption. The assignment is a paper/software investigation, not authorization to commission equipment or a claim that a physical repair was performed.

**Continue:** [Investigate operating histories repair resources and service evidence](Investigate-operating-histories-repair-resources-and-service-evidence.lingua#r_00D184950B7394E9C0B38294BB).

